<p>In 1927, Bertrand Russell delivered a lecture titled <a href="https://users.drew.edu/~jlenz/whynot.html">“Why I Am Not a Christian.”</a> At the time, such a declaration was still fairly bold, as was the reasoning he provided for it. Nearly three decades thereafter, he included in his collection <a href="https://amzn.to/46PM5nT"><em>Portraits from Memory</em></a> another, shorter piece, <a href="https://www.rjgeib.com/thoughts/opiate/why.html">“Why I Am Not a Communist.”</a> That description was safe to apply to oneself in 1954, at least in the mainstream of the English-speaking world.&nbsp;It would have been riskier for those who moved in intellectual circles, as a celebrated philosopher like Russell most certainly did. What’s more, his criticism extended not just to communism as then instantiated by certain societies in the world, but to the thought of <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2018/01/a-short-animated-introduction-to-karl-marx.html">Karl Marx</a> himself.</p>
<p>Russell makes two charges against Marx: “one, that he was muddle-headed; and the other, that his thinking was almost entirely inspired by hatred.” In his view, the author of <em>Capital </em>commits intellectual fraud by opportunistically accepting Thomas Malthus’ dire prediction of population growth (while rejecting it at other times), as well as applying David Ricardo’s theory of value, which describes it as a kind of embodied labor, to wages but not the prices of manufactured goods.</p>
